WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.138
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.138 --> 00:00:02.930
SPEAKER: In order to
prevent global warming

00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:06.500
from reaching 1.5 degrees,
it's estimated that the next 10

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:09.500
years are crucial for us to
collaborate across sectors

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.410
to remove and prevent carbon
pollution, which is also

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:15.320
known as greenhouse gases.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:18.380
An incredibly interdisciplinary
open data project

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:20.240
called Climate
TRACE is presently

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:23.690
led by 10 entities, and former
Vice President, Al Gore,

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:26.300
and is collaborating with
over 50 organizations

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:29.750
to come together and help
detect where carbon pollution is

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:33.080
presently coming from by
country, and even by industry,

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:36.350
using satellite images
and machine learning.

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:38.300
Soon, it will
transparently publish

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:41.480
where pollution arises from
a particular factory, a coal

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:46.100
plant, an agricultural field,
an animal farm, et cetera.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.100
The intention behind creating
this global inventory

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:52.640
of emissions is to help
policymakers, industry

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:55.880
operators, and even
consumers who may unknowingly

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:58.610
be investing in
high-polluting organizations,

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:02.270
and take actions quickly to
prevent a climate crisis.

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:04.849
Because the energy sector
is currently a top focus,

00:01:04.849 --> 00:01:07.280
in this episode,
we walk through how

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:10.940
to generate a geospatial
classification model that

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:13.280
passes the image of
a coal power plant

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:15.680
from a satellite
called Sentinel-2,

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:18.545
and tells us whether
that plant is on or off.

00:01:18.545 --> 00:01:24.680
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:27.860
Thanks to data, today, we can
understand our natural world

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:29.720
with far greater
richness than ever

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:32.300
before by orders of magnitude.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:35.810
This now empowers us to
build amazing apps that

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:39.960
can help us solve complex social
and environmental challenges.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.860
And so in this series,
let's build an app together.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:46.010
Meet Gavin, who is Executive
Director at Watttime, which

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:48.530
is a nonprofit that helps
the public understand

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:51.800
how clean is the electricity
I'm using right now using

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:52.640
technology.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.827
Historically, it's been
challenging to measure this.

00:01:54.827 --> 00:01:56.660
GAVIN MCCORMICK: The
global state of the art

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:59.330
mostly is self-reporting
just asking emitters

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:00.410
how much they emit.

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:02.930
And in some cases, having
extremely expensive

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:06.440
on-site equipment at individual
power plants and factories.

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:09.979
A couple of governments around
the world, but very few,

00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:12.980
have open power
plant monitoring.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.560
And they monitor their
heaviest sources of emissions

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:17.870
and divide that data
free to the public.

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:21.140
For most sources of
emissions around the world,

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.550
there is no equivalent system.

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:25.490
And so it wasn't the
case that we could just

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:28.280
add up all of the open
data and process that

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:29.930
into a global emission system.

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:33.740
We actually needed to go out and
create the emissions databases

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:37.190
that we were aggregating to have
a sense of global emissions.

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:39.350
SPEAKER: Climate TRACE'S
web app and data set

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:41.600
uses publicly available
satellite data

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:45.380
from Earth Engine, as well
as privately owned data.

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:47.900
And all the compute
processing and classifying

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:52.940
of the 59 trillion bytes of
data from over 11,000 sensors

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:55.190
that come from
hundreds of satellites

00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:58.730
is done on Google Cloud by using
products such as TensorFlow

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:01.850
and Vertex AI for training
and labeling data, BigQuery

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:04.700
and Earth Engine for data
analysis, and Kubernetes

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:07.963
Engine for container
management, just to name a few.

00:03:07.963 --> 00:03:10.130
GAVIN MCCORMICK: Without
Google, this project really

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:11.240
would have never happened.

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:14.600
We submitted a grant
application for what we thought

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:17.450
was a little bit of a
pie in the sky moonshot.

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:20.360
We thought if this worked,
it would be amazing.

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:22.610
Google took a really
big bet on a project

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:24.260
back when it was just an idea.

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:26.540
They gave us a
generous $1.7 million

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:28.445
grant to make the
project possible.

00:03:28.445 --> 00:03:29.570
And then they doubled-down.

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:30.290
They said, you know what?

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:32.030
We really want this
project to succeed.

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:35.390
And we're going to send seven
engineers full-time for six

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:37.945
months to help accelerate
this team of scientists

00:03:37.945 --> 00:03:39.320
to make sure the
machine learning

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:41.840
and the data engineering
really are successful.

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:44.870
They then further doubled-down
to support the coalition

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:47.780
as this project matured,
growing from an initial idea

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:50.210
around power plants to
the new Climate TRACE

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:53.300
concept of monitoring
all GHG emissions.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:56.610
SPEAKER: I am beyond impressed
and very proud by this project,

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:59.450
and send a warm-hearted shout
out to all the volunteers

00:03:59.450 --> 00:04:03.590
and organizations who have
made this idea become reality.

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:05.810
For those of us equally
interested in working

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:10.370
with AI and satellite
imagery, let's go a bit deeper

00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:13.580
on how we could
approach this problem.

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:18.110
Let's start with understanding
our inputs and outputs.

00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:20.480
Our input data comes
from Sentinel-2,

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:24.710
which is a satellite owned
by the European Space Agency.

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:27.440
Multi-spectral images
of the entire planet

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:31.610
are taken every 10
days since 2015.

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:34.280
Every pixel in these
images approximately

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:37.580
covers a 10 meter
by 10 meter area.

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:39.410
When working with
satellite data,

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:44.600
each input image has dimensions
of width, height, and bands.

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:47.810
Bands are measurements from
specific satellite instruments

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:51.620
for different ranges of the
electromagnetic spectrum.

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:55.880
For example, Sentinel-2
contains 13 spectral bands.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:58.520
If you are familiar with
image classification problems,

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:01.550
you can think of the
bands similar to images

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:04.800
as red, green,
blue, RGB channels.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:06.630
However, when working
with satellite data,

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:10.010
we generally have more
than just three bands.

00:05:10.010 --> 00:05:12.410
As for outputs, in this
example, the output

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:15.830
is a single number
between 0-off, and 1-on,

00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:20.030
representing the probability
of that coal plant being on.

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:23.090
Next, let's move on to
building a data set.

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:24.740
Climate TRACE went
through the labor

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:28.010
of love of labeling lat-long
locations of coal plants

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:32.570
that correspond to Sentinel-2
images, and shared it as a CSV.

00:05:32.570 --> 00:05:37.580
Each coal plants location is
on or off at different times.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:40.400
Next, we used Earth Engine
to get the band values

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:43.880
for a patch of pixels given
their lat-long timestamp

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:48.800
and on or off label from
each row of the CSV file.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:51.860
We basically view the
band values as the data,

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:54.920
and the label as the answer.

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:57.050
From this, we randomly
split this data

00:05:57.050 --> 00:05:59.930
into training and
validation data sets

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:03.800
and saved them as TF
records in Cloud Storage.

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:07.160
For this case, we split
it into 70% training

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:10.130
and 30% validation data sets.

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:13.370
This means the model studies and
learns from the training data

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:17.900
sets, while the validation
data set is used like an exam

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:20.300
to make sure it's
learning to generalize

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:23.160
to data it hasn't seen before.

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:26.120
Next, we use Vertex AI, Google's
machine learning platform

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:28.430
to train and validate the model.

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:31.140
We use a convolutional
neural network,

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:35.390
which is an ML technique that
works well with image data.

00:06:35.390 --> 00:06:37.520
It receives a patch of
pixels where the power

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:39.710
plant is at its center.

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:42.860
And as a friendly note,
each pixel in Sentinel-2s

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:44.790
is approximately 10 meters.

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:49.560
So we decided to use a padding
of 16 pixels around the plant.

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:55.970
That means that our 33 by 33
patch is around 330 meters

00:06:55.970 --> 00:06:58.160
by 330 meters.

00:06:58.160 --> 00:06:59.900
Because of this,
our training data

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:03.050
is a consistent patch
of 33 by 33 pixels.

00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:06.080
And the model returns the
probability of the plant being

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:07.220
on.

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:10.820
Once we're happy with our model,
it is stored into Cloud Storage

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:12.080
as well.

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:14.870
And in order to use that
model to make predictions

00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:17.480
of coal plants, we
deploy it on Cloud Run,

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:20.030
and make it a
predictions service.

00:07:20.030 --> 00:07:22.685
And when done, we celebrate
with a dance party.

00:07:22.685 --> 00:07:24.416
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:29.135
So what's next?

