00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:41.160
a super passionate peer of mine.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:43.950
You can also find a blog post
and several helpful resources

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:44.670
too.

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:47.070
And community, I'll
leave you with a fun

00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:49.890
fact from Gavin about the
project before we end.

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:51.870
And if you found
this content helpful,

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:54.600
you can subscribe or leave
a comment on other AI

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:57.060
for good use cases
you may have in mind.

00:07:57.060 --> 00:07:57.923
Cheers.

00:07:57.923 --> 00:08:00.090
GAVIN MCCORMICK: One fun
surprise about this project

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:02.490
is, I didn't know how
much the satellites that

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:05.790
orbit the Earth today are
mostly designed for maps.

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:08.940
So that we can all drive
where we need to go,

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:11.250
nearly every satellite
has been designed

00:08:11.250 --> 00:08:14.945
to land at about
11:00 AM overhead.

00:08:14.945 --> 00:08:16.320
Problem is, what
if you're trying

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:17.430
to see the emissions
of something that

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:18.960
doesn't happen at 11:00 AM?

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:22.260
We've had to call
increasingly obscure agencies

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:24.390
around the world of
various countries

00:08:24.390 --> 00:08:26.490
that happen some time to
launch a satellite that's

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:28.950
good at seeing things
at night, and we've

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:32.850
gotten to just how many
satellites are designed to see

00:08:32.850 --> 00:08:33.959
things right before noon.

00:08:33.959 --> 00:08:38.509
[MUSIC PLAYING]

